{
  "id": "Compound::DB01120 - Disease::DOID:9352",
  "verbalization": "Gliclazide, a sulfonylurea antidiabetic agent, is implicated in the pathogenesis of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus through multiple mechanistic pathways. The evidence suggests that Gliclazide may contribute to the development of Type 2 Diabetes by influencing metabolic processes, as it is linked to Epigastric Fullness, Porphyria Hepatic, and Disulfiram Like Reactions, all of which are associated with the broader class of sulfonylurea agents. These conditions are often linked to the treatment of Type 2 Diabetes, indicating a potential role of Gliclazide in the disease process. The shared ancestry with Glyburide and Tolbutamide, both sulfonylureas, underscores the importance of this class in the pathophysiology of the disease. The integrated evidence highlights the role of sulfonylurea agents in modulating glucose regulation and metabolic function, which may contribute to the development of Type 2 Diabetes Mellitus. The mechanisms involving these agents are consistent with their known effects on insulin secretion and glucose homeostasis, supporting a plausible relationship between Gliclazide and the disease.",
